# Conclusion

**Authors:** Lorella Viola

## Abstract
<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title><jats:p>In this final chapter, I review the main formulations of this book, and I retrace the key concepts and values at the foundation of the post-authentic framework that I proposed here. I reflect on why it is no longer sufficient to limit our observations to how the digital is impacting higher education and argue that it is time to ask a more fundamental question. As digital vs non-digital positions have entirely lost relevance, I reiterate that it has become increasingly futile to create ultra-specialised disciplines from other disciplines’ overlapping spaces; this is a legacy of last century’s model of knowledge. The digital transformation has magnified the inadequacy of such a mono-perspective approach and exposed the hypocrisy of a system which praises interdisciplinarity but forces knowledge into rigid categories. I end the chapter with a few additional propositions for remodelling the process of digital knowledge production that could be adopted to restructure academic and higher education programmes not just for today, but also for future generations.</jats:p>

